  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nOverall, the forecast setup remains on track, as previously\nadvertised. A low pressure system will develop off the Carolina\ncoast tonight and lift north on Thursday, occluding over the\nregion. The forecast area will be in the northern deformation\nzone of the low and with the aid of a closed low aloft, a solid\nband of rain will be expected across the area on Thursday,\nleading to around one inch of rain during the day time hours.\nWith the approaching system and its pressure falls, expecting a\nstrong northeast flow to develop with winds on land gusting to\n35 to 45 mph. With stronger winds expected on Lake Erie with the\nunfavorable flow, there is some potential for higher gusts in\nNorthwest Ohio and a non-zero chance of a Wind Advisory.\nHowever, the area could be limited to just the extreme lakeshore\nareas and will allow another forecast cycle to evaluate. This\narea will also be the focus for further evaluation for \nlakeshore flooding as water levels are expected to rise with the\nstrong northeast winds. Have highlighted both of these in the \nHazardous Weather Outlook for the region for now. \n\nAs the low fills in across the area on Thursday night and starts\npushing east on Friday, rain is still expected across the area\nbut will be at a lighter intensity with generally a half inch or\nless for the second half of the event. A lingering trough on\nFriday will try to focus rain in the typical NE OH and NW PA\nsnow belt region with some sub-freezing 850 mb temperatures in\nthis area through Friday night. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nAn new upper trough will eject into the central United States \non Sunday and support the development of a low pressure system. \nThis system will move through the Ohio Valley on Sunday, \nbringing the next round of rain to the area. The strength of the\nlow and the upper trough will allow for rain to be less intense\nthan the Thanksgiving system with rainfall amounts less than \none half of an inch. As this system departs on Monday, much\ncolder air will enter with 850 mb temperatures of -4 to -6C and\nsome of the rain may transition to snow, mainly in NE OH and NW\nPA, where the colder temperatures will be and there may be help\nwith some upslope from the higher hills of the region.",
